Vendored third-party components
===============================

This directory contains third-party code copied ("vendored") into the VpncBar
repository, with local modifications. Both components are licensed GPLv2 (or
later); their full source is included here and our changes are described below
and marked inline in the files.


vendor/vpnc/  — vpnc (IPSec VPN client)
---------------------------------------
Upstream base : https://github.com/streambinder/vpnc   (a maintained fork of
                vpnc 0.5.3, originally by Maurice Massar et al.)
utun port from: https://github.com/breiter/vpnc        (0.5.3-xnu, by Brian Reiter)
License       : GPL v2 (see vendor/vpnc/LICENSE). Includes cisco-decrypt.

Local modifications (to run on Apple Silicon / modern macOS using the kernel's
native "utun" interface instead of the unavailable tuntaposx kext):

  src/sysdep.c
    - Added macOS utun headers (sys/kern_control.h, sys/sys_domain.h,
      net/if_utun.h, sys/uio.h, netinet/ip.h) under __APPLE__.
    - xnu_open_utun(): open a PF_SYSTEM / SYSPROTO_CONTROL socket and connect to
      the "com.apple.net.utun_control" kernel control (no kext required).
    - utun_open(): probe utun units for a free one and return its name (e.g. utun4).
    - utun_read()/utun_write(): handle utun's mandatory 4-byte address-family
      header on every packet.
    - tun_open(): on __APPLE__, prefer native utun; fall back to /dev/tunN.
    (utun implementation ported from breiter/vpnc 0.5.3-xnu.)

  src/sysdep.h
    - Declarations for utun_open / utun_read / utun_write under __APPLE__.

  src/tunip.c
    - get_current_dir_name() shim for macOS/BSD (it is a glibc-only function;
      without this the fork does not compile on macOS).
    - TUN_READ / TUN_WRITE dispatch macros: use utun_read/utun_write when the
      interface name is "utunN", otherwise the classic tun_read/tun_write.

  src/vpnc-disconnect
    - Accept either a pidfile path OR a numeric PID, and verify the target is a
      vpnc process before signalling it. Enables disconnecting an individual
      tunnel when several run at once (one vpnc per profile).

  src/config.c
    - Version banner reports "0.5.3 (utun)".
    - Added a --log-file <path> option (conf directive "LogFile"). When set, all
      console output is redirected to that file (freopen of stdout+stderr), so
      nothing goes to the terminal; logmsg() status/start/stop/error lines are
      written to BOTH the file and syslog, while debug/hex output goes to the file
      only. Lets VpncBar capture a tunnel's full log even though vpnc daemonizes.

  src/tunip.c
    - When --log-file is in effect, the daemonizing fork keeps stdout/stderr on the
      log file instead of redirecting them to /dev/null, and openlog() omits
      LOG_PERROR (so syslog lines aren't duplicated into the file).

  Makefile
    - Dropped GCC-only -freorder-blocks / -fsched-interblock (Apple clang rejects
      them); silenced the pkg-config probe (falls back to libgcrypt-config);
      added -Wno-unused-* to quiet benign upstream warnings.

  Build: make CRYPTO_NONE=yes SCRIPT_PATH=/opt/local/etc/vpnc/vpnc-script
         (certificate support is omitted; we use PSK + XAUTH only.)

  Certificate auth (IKE Authmode cert/hybrid) is OPTIONAL at build time:
    - CRYPTO_NONE=yes          -> no TLS/SSL; PSK + XAUTH only (our default build).
    - (default, no flag)       -> link GnuTLS (LGPL, GPL-compatible) for X.509.
    - OPENSSL_GPL_VIOLATION=yes -> link OpenSSL instead (its license is GPL-
                                  incompatible, hence the flag name; fine for
                                  personal, non-distributed use).
  The core IKE crypto always uses libgcrypt regardless. VpncBar detects whether
  the installed vpnc was built with a TLS backend (via `otool -L`) and only
  offers cert/hybrid auth when it was.


vendor/vpnc-script  — network configuration script
---------------------------------------------------
Upstream : https://gitlab.com/openconnect/vpnc-scripts  (vpnc-script)
License  : GPL v2 or later (see header in the file).

Local modifications (marked inline with "VpncBar policy" comments):

  - Never modify the system default route. set_ipv4_default_route /
    reset_ipv4_default_route (and the IPv6 pair) are overridden to no-ops after
    the OS-specific definitions. VPN split-include routes are still installed, so
    multiple full-tunnel VPNs can coexist without contending for the default route.

  - macOS scoped ("split") DNS only. Register a per-tunnel scoped resolver at
    State:/Network/Service/<utun>/DNS with SupplementalMatchDomains taken from the
    gateway (CISCO_DEF_DOMAIN, CISCO_SPLIT_DNS) plus a VPNC_MATCH_DOMAINS env var
    supplied by VpncBar. Removed the global-DNS takeover the upstream used
    (OverridePrimary, networksetup -setdnsservers, and prepending to the primary
    service DNS). Also dropped the State:/Network/Service/<utun>/IPv4 service
    registration, which configd pruned (taking the scoped DNS with it). If no
    match domain is known, that VPN's DNS is skipped rather than applied globally.
